<h1>Reading notes</h1>
<p class="lede">Written music shows pitch by height: the higher a note sits on the staff, the higher it sounds.
This page names every line and space in the treble and bass clefs, joins them into the grand staff that piano music uses,
goes beyond the staff with ledger lines, and adds sharps and flats. Tap any note to hear it, then finish with the quiz.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>The staff</h2>
<p>A <b>staff</b> is five lines and the four spaces between them. Notes sit on a line or in a space, and each step up —
line to space, space to line — is the next letter of the musical alphabet. There are only seven letters, A B C D E F G;
after G comes A again, one octave higher.</p>
{{> staff clef="treble" notes="C4 D4 E4 F4 G4 A4 B4 C5" name="One step at a time" note="From middle C up to the next C: every step, line to space, is the next letter. (The first C sits on a short extra line of its own — more on that below.)"}}
<figure class="keys-fig" data-from="C4" data-to="C5" data-marks="C4 D4 E4 F4 G4 A4 B4 C5" data-highlight="C4">
  <figcaption><b>The same notes on the piano</b><span>Middle C up to the next C: the white keys, left to right. Tap them to hear.</span></figcaption>
  <div class="keys-art"></div>
</figure>
<p>The sign at the start is a <b>clef</b>. On its own a staff only says “higher” and “lower”; the clef says which line is which note.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>The treble clef</h2>
<p>The treble clef is also called the <b>G clef</b>: its curl wraps around the second line, and marks it as the G above middle C.
It is the clef for higher sounds: the right hand on piano, voices, violin, flute — and guitar, which is written in treble clef
an octave higher than it sounds, to keep it on the staff.</p>
{{> staff clef="treble" notes="E4 G4 B4 D5 F5" name="The lines: E G B D F" note="Every Good Boy Does Fine."}}
{{> staff clef="treble" notes="F4 A4 C5 E5" name="The spaces: F A C E" note="They spell FACE."}}


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>The bass clef</h2>
<p>The bass clef is the <b>F clef</b>: its two dots sit either side of the fourth line, marking it as the F below middle C.
It is the clef for lower sounds: the left hand on piano, bass guitar, cello, trombone and low voices.</p>
{{> staff clef="bass" notes="G2 B2 D3 F3 A3" name="The lines: G B D F A" note="Good Boys Do Fine Always."}}
{{> staff clef="bass" notes="A2 C3 E3 G3" name="The spaces: A C E G" note="All Cows Eat Grass."}}
<p>The same line has a different name in each clef: the bottom line is E in treble but G in bass. Learn each clef as its own map;
don’t try to translate one into the other.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Middle C and the grand staff</h2>
<p>Piano music needs both clefs at once, so it joins a treble staff and a bass staff with a brace: the <b>grand staff</b>.
Usually the right hand reads the top staff and the left hand the bottom one.</p>
<p>Between them sits <b>middle C</b>, the C near the middle of the piano. It is written on a short line of its own — one line
below the treble staff, or one line above the bass staff. Both are the same key. Notes carry on smoothly across the gap: B just
below middle C, D just above it.</p>
{{> staff clef="grand" notes="A3@b B3@b C4@b C4@t D4@t E4@t" labels="names" name="Across the middle" note="Middle C written both ways — the same note."}}
<p>The numbers name the octave. Middle C is <b>C4</b>; each octave counts up from C, so the B just below it is B3 and the D just above it is D4.
It’s the same naming the <a href="note-distance.html">note distance</a> page uses for your do.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Ledger lines</h2>
<p>When a note goes above or below the staff, it gets <b>ledger lines</b>: short extra lines drawn just for that note, as if the staff carried on.
Keep counting line, space, line from the staff and the letters keep going too. Middle C is simply the first ledger line below the treble staff.</p>
{{> staff clef="treble" notes="C4 B3 A3 G3 A5 B5 C6 D6" labels="names" name="Treble, below and above" note="Down from middle C, then up from the A above the staff."}}
{{> staff clef="bass" notes="E2 D2 C2 B3 C4 D4 E4" labels="names" name="Bass, below and above" note="Down from E, then up to middle C and beyond."}}
<p>A few ledger lines are easy to read; many are not. When music climbs far above the staff, it is often written an octave lower
with <i>8va</i> above it, meaning “play this an octave higher”.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Sharps, flats and naturals</h2>
<p>The lines and spaces name the white keys. The black keys borrow a neighbour’s position and add a sign in front of the note:</p>
<ul class="notes">
  <li><b>♯ sharp</b> raises the note a half step: F♯ is the key just above F.</li>
  <li><b>♭ flat</b> lowers it a half step: B♭ is the key just below B.</li>
  <li><b>♮ natural</b> cancels a sharp or flat: back to the plain note.</li>
</ul>
{{> staff clef="treble" notes="F4 F#4 B4 Bb4 Bn4 C5" name="Signs in front" note="F, F sharp · B, B flat, B natural · C."}}
<p>A sharp or flat lasts until the end of the bar, for that line or space. Many pieces put the sharps or flats they use all the time
at the very start of each staff instead — the <b>key signature</b> — which is the subject of another page.</p>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Landmark notes</h2>
<p>Nobody reads notes by counting up from the bottom line every time. Learn a handful of landmarks instead, and read every other note
as a step or two away from the nearest one: the G the treble clef curls around, the F between the bass clef’s dots, middle C,
and the Cs an octave either side of it.</p>
{{> staff clef="grand" notes="C3@b F3@b C4@b C4@t G4@t C5@t" labels="names" name="Landmarks" note="Bass C and F, middle C, treble G and C."}}
<p>The same landmarks on the piano. The black keys come in groups of two and three, and every C is the white key just to the left
of a group of two — that’s how to find any C without counting. Middle C is the C nearest the middle of the keyboard: on a full
88-key piano it is the fourth C from the left, roughly in front of you when you sit at the centre. Tap the keys to hear them.</p>
<figure class="keys-fig" data-from="C3" data-to="C5" data-marks="C3 F3 C4 G4 C5" data-highlight="C4">
  <figcaption><b>Landmarks on the keys</b><span>Each C sits just left of two black keys. Middle C is in red.</span></figcaption>
  <div class="keys-art"></div>
</figure>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Name the note</h2>
<p>A note appears; press its key on the keyboard below — the right key in the right octave, with middle C marked to start from.
Notes can be anywhere on the staff or up to three ledger lines beyond it, and each clef gets the keys it covers.
Start with the treble clef, then the bass clef, then both; tick <b>Sharps and flats</b> once the white keys feel easy.
Speed comes from the landmarks: find the nearest one and count.</p>
<div class="note-quiz" id="note-quiz"></div>


<!-- ================================================================== -->
<h2>Practise it</h2>
<p>A few minutes a day, every day, does more than a long session once a week. Work down the list; each step builds on the one before.</p>
<ol class="notes">
  <li>Say the treble lines and spaces out loud, bottom to top, until you don’t need the sentences: E G B D F, F A C E.</li>
  <li>Do the same for bass: G B D F A, A C E G.</li>
  <li>Run the quiz on one clef until you get ten in a row, then switch to the other clef, then to both.</li>
  <li>Ledger-line notes take longest at first. Read them by counting from the staff, or from middle C, until you know them on sight.</li>
  <li>Take any simple piece and name every note before you play it. Then play it while saying the names.</li>
  <li>Test yourself without looking: which note is on the middle line of the treble staff? On the top line of the bass staff?
  Where is middle C in each clef?
    <details><summary>Answers</summary>B · A · on the first ledger line below the treble staff, or the first ledger line above the bass staff.</details></li>
</ol>

<h2>Worth remembering</h2>
<ul class="notes">
  <li>Higher on the staff sounds higher. Each step, line to space, is the next letter: A B C D E F G, then A again.</li>
  <li>Treble clef: lines E G B D F, spaces F A C E; the curl marks G. Bass clef: lines G B D F A, spaces A C E G; the dots mark F.</li>
  <li>Middle C (C4) is one ledger line below the treble staff or one above the bass staff — the same key.</li>
  <li>♯ raises a half step, ♭ lowers a half step, ♮ cancels; each lasts to the end of the bar.</li>
</ul>

